     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is double the usual maximum of about 2,550 mg per day (2,000 mg for extended-release). A single dose of 1700 mg is also well above the usual maximum of about 1,000 mg. An overdose like this raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be life-threatening. It may be a typo. For example, the intended dose could have been 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 1000 mg twice daily.\n\n2. **A prescriber needs to confirm the order.** Medication orders should come from, or be verified by, a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient. I can't confirm that from here.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. Once I have a corrected order from them, I can enter it right away. If 1700 mg three times daily really was the intent, they should confirm that directly, and pharmacy will probably want to review it as well."
      }
     ],
